1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,248
Two folders, both called cheese_dataset,
with the same file names and the same count.

2
00:00:05,568 --> 00:00:08,277
On Monday you trained a model
on one of them.

3
00:00:08,597 --> 00:00:12,075
On Friday the model got worse,
and nobody touched the code.

4
00:00:12,395 --> 00:00:15,488
So which photos, with which labels,
did that model see?

5
00:00:15,808 --> 00:00:17,813
And can you get exactly those back?

6
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:22,376
Most of us answer with file names:
final, final version 2,

7
00:00:22,376 --> 00:00:26,925
final final version 3,
until nobody knows which one is final.

8
00:00:27,167 --> 00:00:29,770
Here is a better answer: one short line.

9
00:00:30,090 --> 00:00:35,807
In part 2 you will compute this exact string
on your own laptop from 4 cheese photos.

10
00:00:36,127 --> 00:00:40,671
It will match the instructor's tag
in the class repo, character for character.

11
00:00:40,991 --> 00:00:44,959
How can one line pin down
about 600 kilobytes of photos?

12
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:49,488
And before that, when someone clicks gouda,
where does that word actually go?

13
00:00:49,733 --> 00:00:52,228
To follow along you need Docker Desktop,

14
00:00:52,228 --> 00:00:57,093
a Google Cloud project with billing
and your own bucket, and a GitHub account.

15
00:00:57,413 --> 00:01:00,507
This lecture stores a few megabytes
in the cloud,

16
00:01:00,507 --> 00:01:02,085
and gold tags mark where.

17
00:01:02,405 --> 00:01:05,285
The tracker in the top
left follows both READMEs.

18
00:01:05,605 --> 00:01:08,758
A yellow your-turn tag
means pause and run it;

19
00:01:08,758 --> 00:01:12,666
a gray extra tag marks a check
that is not in the README.

20
00:01:12,986 --> 00:01:15,120
Every command is in the study guide.

21
00:01:16,167 --> 00:01:17,276
First, the map.

22
00:01:17,596 --> 00:01:21,701
The lecture draws unlabeled cheese photos,
then labeled ones,

23
00:01:21,701 --> 00:01:24,999
then training,
and the arrow in between asks how.

24
00:01:25,319 --> 00:01:29,692
Labels are the ground truth a model
learns from and is graded against.

25
00:01:30,012 --> 00:01:31,362
The model's mistakes,

26
00:01:31,362 --> 00:01:34,386
once labeled,
go into the next dataset version.

27
00:01:34,634 --> 00:01:37,450
Underneath,
the two stations this lecture builds.

28
00:01:37,770 --> 00:01:43,338
Part 1 is the labeling station: Label Studio
and a small command line container,

29
00:01:43,338 --> 00:01:45,983
with one bucket for photos and labels.

30
00:01:46,303 --> 00:01:50,160
Part 2 is the versioning station:
one DVC container,

31
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:53,767
a bucket with two folders,
your own GitHub repo,

32
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:55,797
and Colab to view versions.

33
00:01:56,117 --> 00:02:00,212
The code does not connect the halves yet;
exercise E7 does.

34
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:02,562
Now, the lecture's recall quiz.

35
00:02:02,882 --> 00:02:06,095
Docker network,
cloud credentials, Docker Compose:

36
00:02:06,095 --> 00:02:08,280
where will each one show up today?

37
00:02:17,117 --> 00:02:21,448
The network lets the command line
container call Label Studio by name.

38
00:02:21,768 --> 00:02:25,821
The credentials are one key file,
mounted into every container.

39
00:02:26,141 --> 00:02:31,261
And Compose is the README's extra section,
which starts both containers together.

40
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:35,575
Clone the data-labeling repo
and move into it; pause and run it.

41
00:02:35,895 --> 00:02:40,304
Give Docker more than 4 gigabytes of memory,
as the README asks;

42
00:02:40,304 --> 00:02:43,404
the setting is now under Settings,
Resources.

43
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:48,973
An extra git log shows this video's commit,
from October 2025;

44
00:02:48,973 --> 00:02:54,296
an extra listing shows the scripts,
cli.py and three pictures.

45
00:02:57,584 --> 00:03:01,894
Next, the secrets folder,
beside the repo and never inside it;

46
00:03:01,894 --> 00:03:03,771
it serves both repos today.

47
00:03:04,091 --> 00:03:07,738
Pause and make it;
an extra tree view matches the README.

48
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:15,245
Now the key.

49
00:03:15,565 --> 00:03:19,872
In the console, create a service account
named data-service-account,

50
00:03:19,872 --> 00:03:21,645
with the Storage Admin role.

51
00:03:21,965 --> 00:03:23,970
Then create one JSON key,

52
00:03:23,970 --> 00:03:28,621
saved in your secrets folder
as data-service-account.json.

53
00:03:28,941 --> 00:03:33,379
This role is broader than needed:
it covers every bucket in the project.

54
00:03:33,699 --> 00:03:35,213
Pause and create yours.

55
00:03:35,450 --> 00:03:38,096
An extra listing shows the key in place:

56
00:03:38,096 --> 00:03:41,338
a stand-in,
explained at the Source storage step.

57
00:03:41,583 --> 00:03:44,921
Download the unlabeled photos
from the README link;

58
00:03:44,921 --> 00:03:47,343
we use curl, and a browser works too.

59
00:03:47,663 --> 00:03:49,092
Then extract the zip.

60
00:03:49,412 --> 00:03:50,692
Pause and do both.

61
00:03:51,012 --> 00:03:55,087
Notice that every image comes
with a twin in a hidden Mac folder.

62
00:03:55,333 --> 00:03:58,469
A quick prediction: the zip held 42 files.

63
00:03:58,789 --> 00:04:01,008
How many tasks should Label Studio make?

64
00:04:05,850 --> 00:04:06,850
21.

65
00:04:07,151 --> 00:04:09,960
An extra count shows 21 images,

66
00:04:09,960 --> 00:04:14,127
one of them a PNG,
plus 21 Mac metadata files.

67
00:04:14,447 --> 00:04:18,415
Upload only the images,
or you get 21 broken tasks.

68
00:04:18,735 --> 00:04:23,301
The lecture calls this curation,
dropping junk before it becomes a task.

69
00:04:23,550 --> 00:04:25,375
One more extra, for part 2:

70
00:04:25,375 --> 00:04:29,161
each photo's fingerprint,
computed from its bytes alone.

71
00:04:29,481 --> 00:04:33,833
Same bytes, same string;
change one byte, and it changes completely.

72
00:04:34,153 --> 00:04:35,561
Remember the first one.

73
00:04:35,950 --> 00:04:38,717
Then create your bucket
with a name of your own,

74
00:04:38,717 --> 00:04:41,369
since names are global,
and keep the defaults.

75
00:04:41,689 --> 00:04:45,567
Add two folders, cheeses_unlabeled
and cheeses_labeled,

76
00:04:45,567 --> 00:04:48,387
and upload the 21 images into the first.

77
00:04:48,707 --> 00:04:50,051
Pause and set it up.

78
00:04:50,371 --> 00:04:54,382
A gold tag: storage starts here,
a fraction of a cent a month.

79
00:04:55,417 --> 00:04:58,982
The README runs Label Studio
from a ready-made image,

80
00:04:58,982 --> 00:05:00,483
in your own container,

81
00:05:00,483 --> 00:05:03,758
so no outside labeling
service sees your photos.

82
00:05:04,078 --> 00:05:07,193
Its script creates the network
only if it is missing,

83
00:05:07,193 --> 00:05:09,838
and removes any old container
with that name.

84
00:05:10,084 --> 00:05:12,537
Then one docker run, flag by flag.

85
00:05:12,857 --> 00:05:15,261
The flag -d runs it in the background,

86
00:05:15,261 --> 00:05:18,361
and the network flag puts it
on a private street.

87
00:05:18,681 --> 00:05:24,975
Then -p 8080:8080 publishes the port:
a door in your laptop wall.

88
00:05:25,295 --> 00:05:29,620
Two -v flags mount a database folder
and your secrets;

89
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:32,825
the -e lines set the login and key path.

90
00:05:34,267 --> 00:05:38,662
Pause and run sh docker-shell.sh;
it pulls the image.

91
00:05:38,982 --> 00:05:42,306
Then the README's check,
docker container ls:

92
00:05:42,306 --> 00:05:44,080
up, port 8080 published.

93
00:05:44,317 --> 00:05:48,946
But look again 25 seconds later,
with an extra -a: exited.

94
00:05:49,266 --> 00:05:51,656
Something to ponder:
where do you look first?

95
00:05:58,500 --> 00:05:59,500
The logs.

96
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:04,409
Label Studio explains itself:
its data folder is not writable.

97
00:06:04,729 --> 00:06:06,307
It runs as user 1001,

98
00:06:06,307 --> 00:06:09,764
but Docker created
the missing folder as root.

99
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:18,769
The fix is an extra, for Linux;
Docker Desktop for Mac normally avoids this.

100
00:06:19,089 --> 00:06:22,366
Give the folder
to user 1001 with sudo chown,

101
00:06:22,366 --> 00:06:25,425
run the script again, and now it stays up.

102
00:06:25,745 --> 00:06:27,879
On Linux, pause and do the same.

103
00:06:29,084 --> 00:06:34,405
Extra checks:
the logs show a box offering version 1.23.2,

104
00:06:34,405 --> 00:06:40,369
newer than this image,
which ran 1.23.0 at the time of recording.

105
00:06:40,689 --> 00:06:43,249
The tag is latest, so yours may differ.

106
00:06:46,150 --> 00:06:49,960
Open localhost:8080 and log
in with the username

107
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:53,851
and password
from lines 19 and 20 of your script.

108
00:06:54,171 --> 00:06:57,820
Create a project
with the Image Classification template,

109
00:06:57,820 --> 00:07:01,275
with the choices brie,
gouda, gruyere and parmigiano.

110
00:07:01,595 --> 00:07:02,769
Pause and do it.

111
00:07:03,089 --> 00:07:06,998
Underneath, our stand-in calls
replay the page's requests:

112
00:07:06,998 --> 00:07:10,705
we are logged in,
and project 1 holds the four cheeses.

113
00:07:13,250 --> 00:07:16,626
Back in the project,
open Settings, then Cloud Storage,

114
00:07:16,626 --> 00:07:19,757
and add source storage,
where the photos come from.

115
00:07:20,077 --> 00:07:24,190
Use the README's values:
Google Cloud Storage, your bucket,

116
00:07:24,190 --> 00:07:27,885
the prefix cheeses_unlabeled,
and pre-signed URLs on.

117
00:07:28,205 --> 00:07:30,573
If a checkbox is missing, carry on.

118
00:07:30,893 --> 00:07:32,660
Leave the credentials empty:

119
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:35,501
that means the key
the container already has.

120
00:07:35,750 --> 00:07:37,414
Then click Check Connection.

121
00:07:37,734 --> 00:07:39,825
Here it fails: file not found.

122
00:07:40,145 --> 00:07:45,129
An extra listing shows why:
/secrets inside Label Studio is empty,

123
00:07:45,129 --> 00:07:49,659
on purpose, as you will hear in a moment;
yours should pass.

124
00:07:49,979 --> 00:07:52,230
The target storage, where labels go,

125
00:07:52,230 --> 00:07:56,358
with the prefix cheeses_labeled,
fails too, with a vaguer message.

126
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:58,904
One honest note about this recording.

127
00:07:59,224 --> 00:08:02,688
This machine has no Google account,
no bucket,

128
00:08:02,688 --> 00:08:05,624
no browser, and no SSH route to GitHub.

129
00:08:05,944 --> 00:08:10,277
The key in the folder listings
is an obviously invalid stand-in,

130
00:08:10,277 --> 00:08:12,579
moved out before every cloud step.

131
00:08:12,899 --> 00:08:17,336
So each cloud step here fails
exactly the way a misplaced key fails.

132
00:08:17,656 --> 00:08:20,301
That is the most common
mistake in this lecture.

133
00:08:20,621 --> 00:08:24,726
Every success you should expect
is tagged recorded, with its source,

134
00:08:24,726 --> 00:08:28,408
and clicks and uploads are replayed locally,
tagged stand-in.

135
00:08:28,650 --> 00:08:32,337
With a real key,
add both storages and click Sync:

136
00:08:32,337 --> 00:08:34,623
one task per photo, 21 for you.

137
00:08:34,943 --> 00:08:38,869
Our stand-in imports 3 tasks
with the same kind of addresses.

138
00:08:39,189 --> 00:08:40,938
Pause, add both, and sync.

139
00:08:41,183 --> 00:08:42,826
So who touches the photos?

140
00:08:43,146 --> 00:08:46,457
Label Studio holds the key
and lists the bucket;

141
00:08:46,457 --> 00:08:48,458
your browser asks for task 1.

142
00:08:48,778 --> 00:08:52,119
Something to ponder:
your browser never sees the key,

143
00:08:52,119 --> 00:08:54,325
so how can it show a private photo?

144
00:09:03,167 --> 00:09:04,468
With a signed ticket.

145
00:09:04,788 --> 00:09:07,424
Label Studio does not relay the photos;

146
00:09:07,424 --> 00:09:12,831
it signs a web address with the key:
permission for one photo, for a short time.

147
00:09:13,151 --> 00:09:15,994
Your browser carries
that ticket straight to Google,

148
00:09:15,994 --> 00:09:17,908
and the key stays in the container.

149
00:09:18,228 --> 00:09:21,983
Then the thumbnails break,
and the README warns you: it is CORS.

150
00:09:25,017 --> 00:09:27,429
The page comes from localhost:8080,

151
00:09:27,429 --> 00:09:31,566
the photo from Google's storage address:
a different origin.

152
00:09:31,886 --> 00:09:36,867
When the page's own code fetches the photo,
as Label Studio does, your browser

153
00:09:36,867 --> 00:09:41,593
hands the answer over only if it carries
a header that allows this origin.

154
00:09:41,913 --> 00:09:46,052
Google adds that header
only if the bucket has its own CORS rule.

155
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:50,011
A small slip:
the README blames a reverse DNS lookup,

156
00:09:50,011 --> 00:09:52,743
but the cause is that same-origin rule.

157
00:09:52,984 --> 00:09:55,821
The README sets the rule
from a second container.

158
00:09:56,141 --> 00:10:00,756
Put your bucket name
on line 23 of docker-shell-CLI.sh,

159
00:10:00,756 --> 00:10:02,435
then run it with sh.

160
00:10:02,755 --> 00:10:05,870
The ID in the prompt changes every run,

161
00:10:05,870 --> 00:10:10,584
and Apple Silicon says aarch64
where this machine says x86.

162
00:10:10,904 --> 00:10:12,184
Pause and do both.

163
00:10:14,550 --> 00:10:18,617
This container joins
the same street and calls Label Studio

164
00:10:18,617 --> 00:10:20,203
by name, not localhost.

165
00:10:20,523 --> 00:10:23,937
It mounts the repo by its folder name,
so keep that name.

166
00:10:24,184 --> 00:10:29,859
Inside, run python cli.py -c, then -m;
pause and run them.

167
00:10:30,179 --> 00:10:32,737
Here both stop at the storage client:

168
00:10:32,737 --> 00:10:35,917
file not found,
because ADC looks for the key.

169
00:10:36,237 --> 00:10:38,838
The code builds one rule, any origin,

170
00:10:38,838 --> 00:10:42,424
GET only, max age one hour,
and patches the bucket.

171
00:10:42,667 --> 00:10:47,488
With a real key, -c prints the rule,
and -m prints the bucket settings.

172
00:10:47,808 --> 00:10:50,582
Now the header arrives
and the thumbnails load.

173
00:10:50,902 --> 00:10:56,278
The max age only caches preflight checks,
which a plain image request never makes.

174
00:10:56,517 --> 00:10:57,861
Something to ponder.

175
00:10:58,181 --> 00:11:02,149
You ran -c and refreshed,
and the thumbnails are still broken.

176
00:11:02,469 --> 00:11:03,877
Name two suspects.

177
00:11:12,717 --> 00:11:14,680
One: the rule missed your bucket.

178
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:19,300
-c failed,
or line 23 names another bucket of yours;

179
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:21,698
-m shows which bucket got it.

180
00:11:22,018 --> 00:11:25,560
Two: your browser reused cached answers;
hard refresh.

181
00:11:25,880 --> 00:11:30,850
And curl never met a bouncer:
CORS is a browser rule, not access control.

182
00:11:31,900 --> 00:11:37,084
Back in Label Studio, click a task,
pick a cheese, and submit; repeat for a few.

183
00:11:37,404 --> 00:11:38,897
Pause and label some.

184
00:11:39,217 --> 00:11:42,851
Here is what one click becomes,
as a stand-in call:

185
00:11:42,851 --> 00:11:46,769
a small JSON document
whose result says choices, gouda.

186
00:11:47,017 --> 00:11:49,513
Label Studio saves it in its database,

187
00:11:49,513 --> 00:11:53,191
and the target storage writes a copy
to cheeses_labeled,

188
00:11:53,191 --> 00:11:56,212
pointing back at the photo,
which never moved.

189
00:11:56,532 --> 00:12:02,200
The slide sorts photos into rows,
but on disk a label is a small JSON note,

190
00:12:02,200 --> 00:12:05,449
and a labeled dataset is photos plus notes.

191
00:12:05,769 --> 00:12:10,121
In the console, that folder
shows one JSON file per annotation.

192
00:12:10,441 --> 00:12:12,126
Open one to see the note.

193
00:12:14,567 --> 00:12:17,895
The command line container
calls Label Studio by name.

194
00:12:18,215 --> 00:12:22,289
Something to ponder:
what if its address were localhost:8080?

195
00:12:29,133 --> 00:12:31,652
It cannot connect: inside a container,

196
00:12:31,652 --> 00:12:35,298
localhost is that container,
and nothing listens there.

197
00:12:35,618 --> 00:12:40,120
The name works, because the Docker network
keeps a phone book of container names.

198
00:12:40,367 --> 00:12:42,471
To list labels from code,

199
00:12:42,471 --> 00:12:46,426
copy your access token
from Account & Settings,

200
00:12:46,426 --> 00:12:51,738
then run the README command:
python cli.py -p -k and your token.

201
00:12:52,058 --> 00:12:53,252
Pause and try it.

202
00:12:53,572 --> 00:12:57,620
On Label Studio 1.23.0 it fails with a 401;

203
00:12:57,620 --> 00:13:04,964
ours says legacy token authentication
has been disabled for this organization.

204
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:09,232
An extra check shows why:
this version has two kinds of token.

205
00:13:09,552 --> 00:13:13,313
The page shows a newer
personal token by default,

206
00:13:13,313 --> 00:13:18,149
the legacy kind is switched off,
and cli.py speaks only legacy.

207
00:13:19,883 --> 00:13:23,706
The fix:
in Organization, API Tokens Settings,

208
00:13:23,706 --> 00:13:25,451
enable legacy tokens.

209
00:13:25,771 --> 00:13:29,056
Then copy the legacy token,
not the personal one.

210
00:13:29,376 --> 00:13:30,657
Pause and do both.

211
00:13:32,900 --> 00:13:36,783
Run -p again, and it lists project 1,
Cheese labels.

212
00:13:37,103 --> 00:13:41,954
Then -t with your token:
3 tasks in our stand-in, 21 for you;

213
00:13:41,954 --> 00:13:44,420
ours has one labeled, as gouda.

214
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:46,041
Pause and run both.

215
00:13:46,361 --> 00:13:49,967
The README's sample says amanita;
the format is the same.

216
00:13:57,800 --> 00:14:02,387
The README's extra section runs
both containers from one compose file.

217
00:14:02,707 --> 00:14:05,785
Leave the command line container with exit,

218
00:14:05,785 --> 00:14:10,152
stop Label Studio, and check with docker ps;
pause and do it.

219
00:14:10,472 --> 00:14:15,720
First put your bucket name
on line 17 of docker-compose.yml as well.

220
00:14:16,040 --> 00:14:18,906
Then run sh docker-shell-compose.sh;

221
00:14:18,906 --> 00:14:24,637
if docker-compose is not found,
use the docker compose form on the card.

222
00:14:24,957 --> 00:14:27,098
Here it stops on a name conflict:

223
00:14:27,098 --> 00:14:31,251
docker stop leaves the container behind,
still holding its name.

224
00:14:31,500 --> 00:14:33,872
An extra docker rm frees the name;

225
00:14:33,872 --> 00:14:37,431
pause, remove it,
and run the compose script again.

226
00:14:37,751 --> 00:14:42,807
This time Compose creates Label Studio
plus a one-off command line container.

227
00:14:44,317 --> 00:14:47,963
An extra echo prints
the README's bucket name:

228
00:14:47,963 --> 00:14:50,131
compose keeps its own copy,

229
00:14:50,131 --> 00:14:54,066
on line 17 of docker-compose.yml,
so edit it too.

230
00:14:54,300 --> 00:14:56,220
And the first call is refused:

231
00:14:56,220 --> 00:14:59,393
the depends_on setting
starts Label Studio first,

232
00:14:59,393 --> 00:15:01,660
but does not wait until it listens.

233
00:15:01,980 --> 00:15:07,228
After an extra wait loop, project 1 is back,
because the same folder is mounted.

234
00:15:10,200 --> 00:15:14,232
Label Studio keeps running
after you exit the command line container.

235
00:15:14,552 --> 00:15:16,210
Then the README's cleanup:

236
00:15:16,210 --> 00:15:19,971
list, stop, prune, and list the images;
pause and run them.

237
00:15:20,291 --> 00:15:25,837
The prune also removed the network,
so next time run docker-shell.sh first.

238
00:15:29,250 --> 00:15:30,575
Something to ponder:

239
00:15:30,575 --> 00:15:34,285
after docker system prune,
is your labeled project gone?

240
00:15:41,133 --> 00:15:45,040
No. The database file
is still in the mounted folder,

241
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:47,512
and prune never touches folders you mount.

242
00:15:47,832 --> 00:15:51,008
But the photos they point
at can change under them:

243
00:15:51,008 --> 00:15:52,440
same name, new picture.

244
00:15:52,760 --> 00:15:55,960
So how do you freeze exactly
which photos a model saw?

245
00:15:56,280 --> 00:15:57,560
That is part 2.

246
00:15:58,600 --> 00:16:01,267
Part 2: freezing which photos a model saw.

247
00:16:01,587 --> 00:16:05,192
Git alone would copy every photo
version into every clone.

248
00:16:05,512 --> 00:16:09,568
Of the lecture's four approaches,
we build the file-based one:

249
00:16:09,568 --> 00:16:14,344
git versions a small file of fingerprints,
and a bucket holds the photos.

250
00:16:14,664 --> 00:16:16,221
The tool is DVC.

251
00:16:18,467 --> 00:16:20,172
First, move up one folder,

252
00:16:20,172 --> 00:16:23,779
so the new clone sits beside
data-labeling and secrets.

253
00:16:24,099 --> 00:16:28,174
The data-versioning README starts
with the same three cleanup commands.

254
00:16:28,494 --> 00:16:29,752
Pause and run them.

255
00:16:34,100 --> 00:16:36,681
Then clone the starter
branch the README names.

256
00:16:37,001 --> 00:16:42,911
Its SSH command needs your key on GitHub,
and this machine has no SSH route.

257
00:16:43,231 --> 00:16:47,689
So the same branch comes over HTTPS,
which the README allows.

258
00:16:48,009 --> 00:16:49,737
Pause, clone it, and move in.

259
00:16:53,350 --> 00:16:56,815
One note: on the day of the lecture
the README changed,

260
00:16:56,815 --> 00:16:58,705
and it now says to clone main.

261
00:16:59,025 --> 00:17:03,155
That branch brings
the instructor's finished DVC files along,

262
00:17:03,155 --> 00:17:05,254
as an extra second clone shows.

263
00:17:05,574 --> 00:17:08,555
This video follows
the course's pinned README:

264
00:17:08,555 --> 00:17:10,694
the starter branch, plus one fix.

265
00:17:10,933 --> 00:17:12,656
Remove the old git history,

266
00:17:12,656 --> 00:17:15,720
so the code is no longer tied
to the class repo,

267
00:17:15,720 --> 00:17:17,610
and start fresh with git init.

268
00:17:17,930 --> 00:17:19,232
Pause and run both.

269
00:17:19,552 --> 00:17:23,925
Git's hint mentions master,
which is why the branch gets renamed next.

270
00:17:25,933 --> 00:17:30,447
On GitHub, create an empty private repo
called data-versioning,

271
00:17:30,447 --> 00:17:33,528
with no README, license or .gitignore file.

272
00:17:33,848 --> 00:17:36,646
Then the five commands: rename the branch,

273
00:17:36,646 --> 00:17:39,778
add your repo as origin,
add, commit, and push.

274
00:17:40,098 --> 00:17:41,357
Pause and run them.

275
00:17:41,677 --> 00:17:44,664
Your repo now shows the 7 files on main.

276
00:17:47,400 --> 00:17:50,152
A bucket stores named objects, nothing else.

277
00:17:50,472 --> 00:17:54,248
Add tools, like versioning,
and the lecture calls it a data lake.

278
00:17:54,568 --> 00:17:58,387
Its folders are only shared name prefixes,
which sets a trap.

279
00:17:59,267 --> 00:18:03,390
The secrets folder
from part 1 already sits beside this repo,

280
00:18:03,390 --> 00:18:05,283
as an extra tree view shows.

281
00:18:05,603 --> 00:18:09,656
The README wants the same service account
and key, so reuse both.

282
00:18:12,883 --> 00:18:16,413
In your bucket,
create two folders in the console:

283
00:18:16,413 --> 00:18:17,896
dvc_store and images.

284
00:18:18,216 --> 00:18:22,516
A small gap: the course's README
never says to put the first photos

285
00:18:22,516 --> 00:18:25,982
into the images folder,
yet later it says to add more.

286
00:18:26,302 --> 00:18:29,374
So upload Image_1
through Image_4 from the zip.

287
00:18:29,694 --> 00:18:30,867
Pause and do it.

288
00:18:33,500 --> 00:18:34,916
In docker-shell.sh,

289
00:18:34,916 --> 00:18:38,492
replace the bucket, project and zone
with yours.

290
00:18:38,812 --> 00:18:40,924
Only the bucket name is ever used.

291
00:18:41,244 --> 00:18:42,503
Pause and edit it.

292
00:18:42,750 --> 00:18:46,270
The entrypoint runs
the four stages the lecture draws.

293
00:18:46,590 --> 00:18:48,169
Authenticate with the key.

294
00:18:48,489 --> 00:18:51,795
Mount the whole bucket as a folder,
with gcsfuse.

295
00:18:52,115 --> 00:18:56,467
Bind its images folder
to the path the app expects, cheese_dataset.

296
00:18:56,787 --> 00:18:57,990
Then use the data:

297
00:18:57,990 --> 00:19:00,998
DVC works on that folder
as if it were local,

298
00:19:00,998 --> 00:19:03,827
while each file is fetched from the bucket.

299
00:19:04,217 --> 00:19:09,209
The lecture takes the mount line apart:
tool, key file, bucket, mount point.

300
00:19:09,529 --> 00:19:12,486
One flag is not passed, --implicit-dirs,

301
00:19:12,486 --> 00:19:16,007
so gcsfuse only shows
folders the console made,

302
00:19:16,007 --> 00:19:18,105
which leave a marker object.

303
00:19:18,425 --> 00:19:19,555
That was the trap.

304
00:19:19,800 --> 00:19:21,534
Note the two bind mounts:

305
00:19:21,534 --> 00:19:24,654
-v joins your laptop's repo
to the container,

306
00:19:24,654 --> 00:19:28,141
and the inner one joins
the bucket's images folder.

307
00:19:28,383 --> 00:19:31,455
Mounting needs the fuse
device and mount rights,

308
00:19:31,455 --> 00:19:34,847
hence the --privileged flag
and the extra capability.

309
00:19:35,167 --> 00:19:38,708
The script also mounts
your repo, secrets and git config.

310
00:19:39,028 --> 00:19:43,487
Set up git before the first run,
or Docker creates a folder in its place.

311
00:19:44,533 --> 00:19:47,968
You are already
in the data-versioning folder,

312
00:19:47,968 --> 00:19:49,909
so run sh docker-shell.sh.

313
00:19:50,229 --> 00:19:51,381
Pause and run it.

314
00:19:51,701 --> 00:19:54,439
On the starter branch, the build dies:

315
00:19:54,439 --> 00:19:58,400
package google-cloud-sdk
has no installation candidate.

316
00:19:58,720 --> 00:20:02,730
The message even names the replacement,
google-cloud-cli.

317
00:20:02,966 --> 00:20:07,056
A small slip, and not your fault:
Google renamed the package,

318
00:20:07,056 --> 00:20:11,414
the main branch fixed line 36,
and the starter never got the fix.

319
00:20:11,734 --> 00:20:14,486
Make the same one-word change, as an extra.

320
00:20:14,806 --> 00:20:16,065
Pause and edit it.

321
00:20:17,283 --> 00:20:18,755
Pause and run it again.

322
00:20:19,075 --> 00:20:20,632
The first build is slow.

323
00:20:20,952 --> 00:20:22,510
Then the entrypoint runs,

324
00:20:22,510 --> 00:20:25,624
and every stage fails
the way a missing key fails.

325
00:20:25,944 --> 00:20:28,813
The gcloud tool cannot find the key file,

326
00:20:28,813 --> 00:20:32,451
and neither can gcsfuse,
so the bucket never mounts.

327
00:20:32,771 --> 00:20:37,294
Yet the next line says bucket mounted,
because the script prints it no matter what.

328
00:20:37,614 --> 00:20:41,368
Then the bind mount fails:
special device does not exist.

329
00:20:41,616 --> 00:20:43,526
And still you get a root prompt,

330
00:20:43,526 --> 00:20:46,331
because the entrypoint
does not stop at errors.

331
00:20:46,651 --> 00:20:51,263
Extra checks:
the cheese_dataset folder is empty,

332
00:20:51,263 --> 00:20:56,251
and DVC 3.63.0 is installed,
pinned by the lock file.

333
00:20:56,571 --> 00:20:59,984
So the rule for today:
list the folder before you add it.

334
00:21:00,233 --> 00:21:03,902
With a real key,
the folder inside lists your four photos.

335
00:21:04,222 --> 00:21:08,190
Something to ponder:
on your laptop, the same folder is empty.

336
00:21:08,510 --> 00:21:09,918
Is the mount broken?

337
00:21:18,766 --> 00:21:22,713
No.
Both mounts exist only inside the container.

338
00:21:23,033 --> 00:21:28,128
Your laptop sees the empty folder
the entrypoint made through the repo mount;

339
00:21:28,128 --> 00:21:30,841
an extra listing shows it, owned by root.

340
00:21:34,400 --> 00:21:37,323
Inside the container, run dvc init.

341
00:21:37,643 --> 00:21:39,084
On Linux it may stop:

342
00:21:39,084 --> 00:21:42,720
the repository path
is not owned by the current user.

343
00:21:43,040 --> 00:21:46,215
You are root inside,
the repo is yours on disk,

344
00:21:46,215 --> 00:21:49,120
so git refuses, leaving a half-made folder.

345
00:21:49,367 --> 00:21:54,316
The fix is an extra, for Linux:
on your laptop, remove the half-made files.

346
00:21:54,636 --> 00:21:58,348
Then mark /app as a safe
directory in your git config.

347
00:21:58,668 --> 00:22:01,890
Start the container again,
and dvc init works.

348
00:22:02,210 --> 00:22:03,361
Pause and run it.

349
00:22:04,583 --> 00:22:08,510
If you cloned main, it stops here:
DVC is already set up,

350
00:22:08,510 --> 00:22:12,988
the remote is refused,
and the config points at the class bucket.

351
00:22:13,308 --> 00:22:14,695
Start from the starter.

352
00:22:14,933 --> 00:22:20,586
Then point DVC at the dvc_store folder
in your bucket, as its default remote.

353
00:22:20,906 --> 00:22:22,059
Pause and add it.

354
00:22:24,900 --> 00:22:27,716
Here is what dvc add will do with photos.

355
00:22:28,036 --> 00:22:30,681
It fingerprints each photo with MD5.

356
00:22:31,001 --> 00:22:34,189
It files a copy in its cache
under that fingerprint,

357
00:22:34,189 --> 00:22:36,825
on a shelf named by the first 2 characters.

358
00:22:37,145 --> 00:22:41,144
Then it fingerprints a manifest,
the list of fingerprints and names,

359
00:22:41,144 --> 00:22:44,084
and writes that fingerprint
into the pointer file,

360
00:22:44,084 --> 00:22:46,319
which stands in for the folder in git.

361
00:22:48,233 --> 00:22:52,238
A second trap: the mount failed,
so the folder is empty,

362
00:22:52,238 --> 00:22:55,956
and dvc add happily makes a version
with zero files.

363
00:22:56,717 --> 00:22:58,163
With no bucket mounted,

364
00:22:58,163 --> 00:23:01,622
our stand-in copies four photos
into the laptop folder,

365
00:23:01,622 --> 00:23:04,648
which the container sees
through the repo mount,

366
00:23:04,648 --> 00:23:05,720
and we add again.

367
00:23:06,040 --> 00:23:10,498
Now the pointer file says 4 files
and one fingerprint for the folder.

368
00:23:10,818 --> 00:23:12,205
Pause and add yours.

369
00:23:16,250 --> 00:23:19,962
The cache shows those shelves,
plus the empty version from before.

370
00:23:24,667 --> 00:23:29,851
So one line pins 600 kilobytes:
it fingerprints the list of fingerprints.

371
00:23:30,171 --> 00:23:34,492
And the instructor's tag in the class
repo holds the same fingerprint,

372
00:23:34,492 --> 00:23:35,973
character for character.

373
00:23:37,317 --> 00:23:38,661
Something to ponder.

374
00:23:38,981 --> 00:23:42,181
You give Image_2 the new name brie_2,
and add again.

375
00:23:42,501 --> 00:23:47,173
Which changes: the photo fingerprint,
the manifest fingerprint, the size,

376
00:23:47,173 --> 00:23:48,325
or the file count?

377
00:23:57,167 --> 00:24:01,028
Only the manifest fingerprint,
because one name in the list changed.

378
00:24:01,348 --> 00:24:05,572
The opening's two folders were the reverse:
same names, new bytes.

379
00:24:05,892 --> 00:24:09,210
And DVC stores whole files, not differences;

380
00:24:09,210 --> 00:24:13,508
one changed pixel means a new fingerprint
and a new copy.

381
00:24:15,150 --> 00:24:16,814
Next, dvc push.

382
00:24:17,134 --> 00:24:18,286
Pause and run it.

383
00:24:18,606 --> 00:24:20,633
Here it fails without credentials.

384
00:24:20,953 --> 00:24:22,964
To show what a push uploads,

385
00:24:22,964 --> 00:24:26,269
an extra push goes
to a local stand-in remote:

386
00:24:26,269 --> 00:24:29,742
5 files pushed,
the four photos and the manifest.

387
00:24:29,983 --> 00:24:33,311
In your bucket,
the dvc_store now mirrors the cache.

388
00:24:33,631 --> 00:24:38,559
A gold tag: a second copy of the photos,
still a fraction of a cent a month.

389
00:24:38,879 --> 00:24:43,848
So the bytes now sit in three places:
the images folder you upload to,

390
00:24:43,848 --> 00:24:47,114
and the cache on your disk,
filled by dvc add.

391
00:24:47,434 --> 00:24:49,250
The third is the dvc_store,

392
00:24:49,250 --> 00:24:52,276
where every file is named
by its fingerprint.

393
00:24:52,596 --> 00:24:54,558
The images folder can change;

394
00:24:54,558 --> 00:24:58,954
the dvc_store never overwrites,
because new bytes get a new name.

395
00:24:59,200 --> 00:25:01,333
Now git, outside the container.

396
00:25:01,653 --> 00:25:06,588
Here, git status shows the DVC files
that dvc init already staged,

397
00:25:06,588 --> 00:25:09,653
the new pointer file, and your two edits.

398
00:25:09,973 --> 00:25:13,577
Add and commit:
6 small text files and no photos,

399
00:25:13,577 --> 00:25:17,696
because the .gitignore file
excludes the dataset folder.

400
00:25:17,933 --> 00:25:21,481
Tag the commit as dataset_v20,
a permanent name,

401
00:25:21,481 --> 00:25:27,320
and push the branch and the tag together;
atomic means both arrive, or neither.

402
00:25:27,640 --> 00:25:28,898
Pause and run them.

403
00:25:29,218 --> 00:25:32,909
An extra log shows the tag,
which pins these six lines.

404
00:25:35,033 --> 00:25:37,905
An extra diff against the instructor's tag:

405
00:25:37,905 --> 00:25:41,646
identical, same bytes, same fingerprint,
on any machine.

406
00:25:41,966 --> 00:25:43,340
That is the whole trick:

407
00:25:43,340 --> 00:25:46,318
git keeps the fingerprint
of a list of fingerprints.

408
00:25:46,638 --> 00:25:49,732
A rope leads from the tag
to the bytes in your bucket.

409
00:25:49,967 --> 00:25:52,908
The README warns that this tag may be taken;

410
00:25:52,908 --> 00:25:56,986
that applies only to a fork,
and your fresh repo has no tags.

411
00:25:57,306 --> 00:26:01,801
Its table sums it up:
git holds code and small DVC files,

412
00:26:01,801 --> 00:26:06,138
DVC tracks the dataset,
the bucket stores the versions.

413
00:26:06,458 --> 00:26:09,603
The README now opens Colab for version 20;

414
00:26:09,603 --> 00:26:13,796
we'll do that once,
for both versions, after version 21.

415
00:26:16,033 --> 00:26:17,505
Now a second version.

416
00:26:17,825 --> 00:26:21,057
Upload a few more photos
to the images folder;

417
00:26:21,057 --> 00:26:23,585
our stand-in copies in one, Image_5.

418
00:26:23,905 --> 00:26:26,671
With a real bucket,
the mount can take a little

419
00:26:26,671 --> 00:26:29,025
while to notice, so list before you add.

420
00:26:29,345 --> 00:26:31,222
Pause and upload one or two.

421
00:26:31,466 --> 00:26:32,810
Something to ponder.

422
00:26:33,130 --> 00:26:37,141
Four photos are already in the dvc_store,
and you add one.

423
00:26:37,461 --> 00:26:40,213
How many objects will dvc push upload?

424
00:26:49,050 --> 00:26:51,461
Two: the new photo and a new manifest.

425
00:26:51,781 --> 00:26:56,175
Start the container again, list the folder,
and add it; pause and run them.

426
00:26:56,495 --> 00:26:59,738
It gives 5 files
and a new manifest fingerprint.

427
00:27:00,058 --> 00:27:05,476
The class version 21 has a different one,
because its fifth photo was another image.

428
00:27:09,216 --> 00:27:11,840
Then dvc push; pause and run it.

429
00:27:12,160 --> 00:27:14,982
Here the local stand-in
shows 2 files pushed,

430
00:27:14,982 --> 00:27:18,304
because the four old photos
are already on the shelf.

431
00:27:18,624 --> 00:27:21,248
That is the saved space
the lecture promised.

432
00:27:21,483 --> 00:27:25,260
Back on your laptop,
the five git commands again:

433
00:27:25,260 --> 00:27:32,043
status shows only the pointer file changed;
then add, commit, tag dataset_v21, and push.

434
00:27:32,363 --> 00:27:33,622
Pause and run them.

435
00:27:33,942 --> 00:27:37,099
An extra diff shows the whole change
in three lines.

436
00:27:43,366 --> 00:27:48,294
The starter branch keeps an older README
that adds the remote again at this point.

437
00:27:48,614 --> 00:27:51,558
That fails: remote origin already exists.

438
00:27:51,878 --> 00:27:53,756
The pinned README says not to.

439
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:58,189
One extra experiment, to watch,
not to run on a real mount:

440
00:27:58,189 --> 00:28:02,733
on our stand-in folder,
check out the old pointer file with git,

441
00:28:02,733 --> 00:28:06,779
run dvc checkout,
and the folder holds four photos again.

442
00:28:07,099 --> 00:28:10,107
Back to main, and five,
from the cache on disk.

443
00:28:10,427 --> 00:28:14,272
With a real mount,
this folder is your bucket's images folder,

444
00:28:14,272 --> 00:28:17,125
so checkout deletes and rewrites
photos there.

445
00:28:21,300 --> 00:28:23,273
Now, time travel from anywhere:

446
00:28:23,273 --> 00:28:26,647
the lecture's phrase
for reaching an earlier version,

447
00:28:26,647 --> 00:28:29,321
which works only while its files are kept.

448
00:28:29,641 --> 00:28:33,097
The command dvc get,
with a tag, walks the rope.

449
00:28:33,417 --> 00:28:38,409
It clones the repo at that tag, reads
that commit's config to find the bucket,

450
00:28:38,409 --> 00:28:41,161
then fetches the manifest, then the photos.

451
00:28:41,400 --> 00:28:44,536
Open the notebook from the README
and save a copy.

452
00:28:44,856 --> 00:28:49,656
Install DVC, load the libraries,
and sign in with your Google account.

453
00:28:49,976 --> 00:28:53,325
Then list a repo, fetch a version,
and count the files.

454
00:28:53,567 --> 00:28:56,141
Our stand-in is not signed in,

455
00:28:56,141 --> 00:29:01,546
and pip gives it a newer DVC, 3.67.1,
at the time of recording.

456
00:29:01,866 --> 00:29:05,746
Listing the class repo fails with a 401,
and look where:

457
00:29:05,746 --> 00:29:09,399
at the manifest,
because DVC fetches the list first,

458
00:29:09,399 --> 00:29:10,804
in the rope's order.

459
00:29:13,517 --> 00:29:18,274
The instructor's signed-in run
lists five files on the class repo,

460
00:29:18,274 --> 00:29:21,517
the same set as its version 21,
and counts 5.

461
00:29:21,837 --> 00:29:25,421
Your account probably
cannot access the class bucket,

462
00:29:25,421 --> 00:29:26,570
so use your repo:

463
00:29:26,570 --> 00:29:30,370
replace the address and the tag;
your version 20 gives 4.

464
00:29:30,690 --> 00:29:32,023
Your repo is private,

465
00:29:32,023 --> 00:29:35,640
so put an access token in the address,
or make it public.

466
00:29:35,960 --> 00:29:37,453
Pause and work through it.

467
00:29:38,150 --> 00:29:39,494
Something to ponder.

468
00:29:39,814 --> 00:29:46,214
Cell 19 asks quietly for dataset_v25,
and prints nothing. Did it work?

469
00:29:53,050 --> 00:29:56,527
No. The next cell fails: no such folder.

470
00:29:56,847 --> 00:30:00,986
Without the --quiet flag, the error appears:
unknown Git revision.

471
00:30:01,306 --> 00:30:05,893
The class repo only
has tags v20, v21 and v30.

472
00:30:06,213 --> 00:30:09,605
The stored count of 3 is left
over from an older run;

473
00:30:09,605 --> 00:30:11,781
the --quiet flag hides errors too.

474
00:30:13,817 --> 00:30:16,804
A quick prediction:
three questions, three tools.

475
00:30:17,124 --> 00:30:20,900
Match Delta Lake or Iceberg, DVC,
and lakeFS.

476
00:30:25,734 --> 00:30:28,913
DVC, lakeFS, then the table formats.

477
00:30:29,233 --> 00:30:30,937
There is no single standard;

478
00:30:30,937 --> 00:30:34,224
your project milestone
accepts a light tool like this.

479
00:30:34,467 --> 00:30:36,675
The lecture ends with monitoring:

480
00:30:36,675 --> 00:30:40,355
watch quality,
freshness, drift, cost and broken steps.

481
00:30:40,675 --> 00:30:45,070
Versioning tells you what changed;
monitoring tells you that something changed.

482
00:30:45,317 --> 00:30:50,565
On one map: Label Studio runs in a container
and signs tickets for the photos.

483
00:30:50,885 --> 00:30:54,256
A rule on the bucket satisfies
the browser's bouncer.

484
00:30:54,576 --> 00:30:58,458
Labels are small notes;
a mounted bucket feeds DVC.

485
00:30:58,778 --> 00:31:02,490
Photos are filed by fingerprint;
a tag pins the list.

486
00:31:02,810 --> 00:31:06,536
So Monday's photos come back
with one dvc get;

487
00:31:06,536 --> 00:31:09,210
their labels join in exercise E7.

488
00:31:09,450 --> 00:31:11,455
5 questions to test yourself.

489
00:31:11,775 --> 00:31:16,298
One: who fetches the photos,
and why does the bucket need its CORS rule?

490
00:31:16,618 --> 00:31:21,290
Two: why can't the command line container
and your browser swap addresses?

491
00:31:21,610 --> 00:31:25,514
Three: what is in the pointer file,
and what changes on a rename?

492
00:31:25,834 --> 00:31:28,159
Four: where do the bytes sit after a push?

493
00:31:28,479 --> 00:31:32,512
Five: without the dvc_store,
what does dvc get do?

494
00:31:37,534 --> 00:31:41,075
Cleanup is the README's three commands;
pause and run them.

495
00:31:41,395 --> 00:31:45,449
If you keep the labeling bucket,
remove its CORS rule with -r.

496
00:31:45,769 --> 00:31:48,576
Buckets cost money until you delete them,

497
00:31:48,576 --> 00:31:52,939
but never delete the dvc_store
while any tag should still work;

498
00:31:52,939 --> 00:31:54,601
a tag is only a pointer.

499
00:31:54,921 --> 00:31:58,632
Delete the key and its JSON file
when the course is done.

500
00:31:58,867 --> 00:32:00,915
Answers are in the exercises.

501
00:32:01,235 --> 00:32:03,814
Lecture 13 trains from a prepared zip;

502
00:32:03,814 --> 00:32:07,208
a tagged version would make
that run reproducible.

